1
00:00:00,817 --> 00:00:05,042
USGLoc: uncertainty-aware visual localization.

2
00:00:06,650 --> 00:00:10,925
A robot fuses every camera pose into its state estimator.

3
00:00:11,275 --> 00:00:15,025
Fusion is only as good as the uncertainty of each pose.

4
00:00:15,375 --> 00:00:20,025
Yet most localizers return a single pose, with no
confidence.

5
00:00:20,375 --> 00:00:23,000
How much should the robot trust this pose?

6
00:00:24,608 --> 00:00:28,433
Regressors such as Marepo and ACE return one pose.

7
00:00:28,783 --> 00:00:32,583
So do splatting localizers, such as GSplatLoc.

8
00:00:32,933 --> 00:00:37,883
To our knowledge, no splatting localizer returns a pose
distribution.

9
00:00:40,283 --> 00:00:44,808
USGLoc keeps one uncertainty channel, from the map to the
pose.

10
00:00:45,158 --> 00:00:51,008
Every Gaussian carries a Dempster-Shafer belief, with a
vacuity mass for missing evidence.

11
00:00:51,358 --> 00:00:57,058
The query passes through the same head, and vacuity gates
matching into a few hypotheses.

12
00:00:57,408 --> 00:01:02,133
A render-and-match correction then snaps each hypothesis
into place.

13
00:01:02,483 --> 00:01:07,033
Rendered like color, vacuity also weights the refinement per
pixel.

14
00:01:07,383 --> 00:01:15,558
A tempered Laplace approximation turns each hypothesis into
a covariance: a Gaussian mixture over SE(3).

15
00:01:18,192 --> 00:01:22,117
Demo one: Stairs, our best case on 7Scenes.

16
00:01:22,467 --> 00:01:26,467
Top: the query, and the map rendered at each method's pose.

17
00:01:26,817 --> 00:01:31,492
Bottom: a third-person view of the map, with our two-sigma
covariance.

18
00:01:31,842 --> 00:01:39,642
Here our median error is 1.5 centimeters, against 2.9 for
ACE and 3.4 for GSplatLoc.

19
00:01:39,992 --> 00:01:45,192
On the queries where we fail, our reported uncertainty is 15
times larger.

20
00:01:47,817 --> 00:01:52,242
Demo two: Apartment 2 Kitchen, our best case on 12Scenes.

21
00:01:52,592 --> 00:02:00,767
Our median error is 0.4 centimeters, against 0.6 for ACE and
0.7 for GSplatLoc.

22
00:02:01,117 --> 00:02:07,517
Across the 12 scenes, USGLoc is more accurate than every
baseline shown on 7 of them.

23
00:02:09,750 --> 00:02:12,750
Demo three: what the uncertainty is worth.

24
00:02:13,100 --> 00:02:16,700
We keep the half of the queries each method trusts most.

25
00:02:17,050 --> 00:02:30,200
Our median error drops from 0.77 to 0.50 centimeters; ACE
drops to 0.60 with a PnP covariance, and to 0.70 with its
inlier count.

26
00:02:30,550 --> 00:02:34,525
The poses USGLoc trusts most are the most accurate.

27
00:02:36,150 --> 00:02:44,525
On 7Scenes, USGLoc has the lowest average error and the
highest success rate at two centimeters and two degrees.

28
00:02:44,875 --> 00:02:49,450
And it is the only method here that reports how much to
trust its pose.

29
00:02:51,067 --> 00:02:54,842
USGLoc: a pose, and how much to trust it.

30
00:02:55,192 --> 00:02:57,967
Code and video are linked in the paper.
